it may not get the same recognition as  ve day  but vga day was every bit as significant  with germany defeated and hitler dead  japan held firm  and the war continued in the pacific  until the united states dropped atomic  bombs on two japanese cities  forcing their surrender seventy-five  years on  roy miller remembers the campaign in the  far east vividly  1943 was lamb scotland he joined the  royal navy at the age of 15  and ended up on hms indomitable  responsible for shooting down kamikaze  pilots  my job was to defend her if she was  attacked and from time to time  they did come at us with kamikaze  attacks  that wasn't really pleasant because you  had to shoot them down otherwise they're  going to blow into you and knock you  out we had several nasty experiences  and we knocked a few of them down and  there is a trickle of fear runs through  you  you know he's going to get you always  after you he's going to hit the ship  he's going to do it he's got to be shot  down you've got to kick you're going to  go next to him  you've got to keep your nerve roy says  that on august 15  1945 for him there was no euphoria  just one very distinct feeling it wasn't  joy at all it was a relief  to really realize that no you know this  was an end  i mean some people say well don't you  don't you celebrate  i didn't celebrate anything it was a  feeling of relief  that the whole thing was coming to an  end  the allied effort in the far east was  very much a multicultural one  with troops from all over the  commonwealth taking part in what was at  times  brutal combat the japanese were the most  spiritual fighters under the sun  they believed that when you are captured  as a prisoner in a war you have  disgraced your entire family  but when you die  it's a great honor to the whole family  you can just imagine such a mentality  more than 70  000 british and commonwealth troops died  in the war against japan  among them more than twelve thousand  prisoners of war who died in japanese  captivity  roy is 96 now and says he will mark the  day by remembering those he served with  who weren't so fortunate and the brady  sky  news
 

